            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Pauling as a Peace Activist</strong><br>\nBeyond his scientific achievements, Linus Pauling was a passionate advocate for peace. He was an outspoken critic of nuclear weapons testing and worked tirelessly to promote international disarmament. His activism was recognized when he was awarded the Nobel Peace Prize in 1962, making him one of the few individuals to receive two separate Nobel Prizes.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Vitamin C and Health</strong><br>\nLater in his career, Pauling became interested in the health benefits of vitamin C. He published several papers and books suggesting that high doses of vitamin C could prevent colds and other diseases. While his views were controversial and not widely accepted by the medical community, they spurred significant interest and research into the role of vitamins in human health.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Criticism and Controversy</strong><br>\nPauling's advocacy for vitamin C faced criticism from the scientific community. Many experts argued that his claims were not supported by sufficient evidence and criticized him for promoting vitamin C without rigorous scientific proof. Despite this controversy, Pauling remained a firm believer in the health benefits of vitamin C until his death.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
