            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Personal Life and Legacy</strong><br>\nPauling was married to Ava Helen Miller in 1923, and they had four children together. His personal life was marked by his commitment to family and social causes. After his death in 1994, Pauling left behind a legacy as a pioneering scientist and a dedicated peace activist, remembered for his charisma and unyielding optimism about science and humanity's potential.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Honors and Awards</strong><br>\nIn addition to his two Nobel Prizes, Pauling received numerous other awards and honors throughout his lifetime. He was a member of the National Academy of Sciences and received over 40 honorary degrees from universities around the world. His work remains highly influential in various scientific fields.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Linus Pauling's Impact on Education</strong><br>\nPauling's work has greatly impacted science education. His textbooks, such as \"General Chemistry,\" have been used in classrooms for decades, shaping how chemistry is taught. He also advocated for a broader science curriculum that emphasized critical thinking and problem-solving, influencing educational practices beyond chemistry.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
